d using the polar Laplacian and angular integration by parts gives $(r^2f_{lm}')'+(k^2r^2-l(l+1))f_{lm}=0$ distributionally. Consequently $\\alpha_{lm}''+(k^2-l(l+1)r^{-2})\\alpha_{lm}=0$ on $(R,\\infty)$. One-dimensional $H^2_{\\mathrm{loc}}$ functions have $C^1$ representatives; this equation with smooth coefficients then gives a $C^2$ representative and iterated differentiation gives smoothness. This requires no extra regularity of $V$.",
